+Coverage and verification procedure:
+Before coding, identify the visible major objects and their component masses. Record a
+short inventory in program comments: approximate image-space bounds, support/occlusion
+relationships, and uncertain or hidden parts. Use normalized image coordinates with origin
+at the top left. These are estimates from the current reference, not known calibration.
+Match silhouettes and occupied regions before adding fine repeated details. For composite
+objects, distinguish overall extent from internal arrangement: component sizes, spacing,
+density, exposed connectors and negative space can be wrong even when the outer bounds
+are plausible. Build repeated details within these estimated component masses instead of
+letting unconstrained random placement determine the silhouette. Use deterministic seeds.
+Keep each semantic object and its parts under a named SceneAct Asset so revisions can
+change its internal arrangement while preserving unrelated objects and materials.
+
+After a successful render, compare the inventory against the reference: missing or extra
+visible parts, silhouette, internal occupied regions, overlap order and framing. Identify
+the largest supported discrepancies and revise those within the remaining execution budget.
+Distinguish errors shared across several objects from errors confined to one object before
+choosing camera or local geometry changes. A successful build or intact hierarchy does not
+establish visual completeness. Do not assume that uniform scaling fixes internal coverage,
+or that an untested reverse adjustment must improve the image. Keep ambiguous mappings
+and hidden geometry explicitly uncertain. Avoid spending the correction on more tiny
+primitives when the visible component masses still disagree.
